WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.834
We introduce JUICE:

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:03.884
one process for images and text

00:00:03.884 --> 00:00:06.021
in pretrained encoder spaces.

00:00:06.071 --> 00:00:09.618
Why do most unified models split images and text?

00:00:09.618 --> 00:00:11.681
A language model writes the text,

00:00:11.681 --> 00:00:14.571
while an attached diffusion head draws the image.

00:00:14.621 --> 00:00:15.986
We take another route:

00:00:15.986 --> 00:00:17.386
one process for both,

00:00:17.386 --> 00:00:19.132
learned together from scratch.

00:00:19.182 --> 00:00:22.047
Frozen encoders, pretrained at scale,

00:00:22.047 --> 00:00:25.022
map images and text into continuous states,

00:00:25.022 --> 00:00:27.490
each with its own noise scale and time.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.887
One flow matching Transformer, trained from scratch,

00:00:30.887 --> 00:00:33.362
gives each modality its own projections

00:00:33.362 --> 00:00:34.994
and one joint attention.

00:00:35.044 --> 00:00:36.592
Time tokens tell the model

00:00:36.592 --> 00:00:38.867
which inputs are clean conditions

00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:40.535
and which are being generated.

00:00:40.585 --> 00:00:43.958
Frozen decoders map them back to pixels and words,

00:00:43.958 --> 00:00:46.601
decoding text in one parallel pass.

00:00:46.651 --> 00:00:49.236
Which inputs stay clean defines the task.

00:00:49.286 --> 00:00:52.286
Two times, one per modality, span a square.

00:00:52.336 --> 00:00:55.322
Text clean, image noisy: that's text-to-image.

00:00:55.372 --> 00:00:57.214
Image clean, text noisy:

00:00:57.214 --> 00:00:59.650
that's captioning, or answering questions.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.485
Both noisy: we generate them together.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:03.752
With one model,

00:01:03.752 --> 00:01:06.108
every task is a path through this square.

00:01:06.158 --> 00:01:07.880
Our training covers both:

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.767
conditional denoising on the edges,

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.548
joint denoising inside.

00:01:11.598 --> 00:01:13.863
We ran controlled pretraining studies,

00:01:13.863 --> 00:01:15.550
one change at a time.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.399
First: continuous text, or tokens?

00:01:18.449 --> 00:01:20.521
Against three token designs,

00:01:20.521 --> 00:01:24.396
continuous text leads image generation throughout training,

00:01:24.396 --> 00:01:28.242
but its CIDEr ends just over half that of autoregressive text.

00:01:28.292 --> 00:01:31.684
Continuous text spares images, but costs wording.

00:01:31.734 --> 00:01:34.151
Second: which tasks should we train on?

00:01:34.234 --> 00:01:37.001
Adding joint denoising to the conditional tasks

00:01:37.001 --> 00:01:39.743
leads on every metric, throughout training.

00:01:39.793 --> 00:01:41.127
Train where you sample:

00:01:41.127 --> 00:01:43.145
on the edges, and in the interior.

00:01:43.195 --> 00:01:45.866
Third: which image space should the model use?

00:01:45.916 --> 00:01:50.914
Semantic encoders beat VAE latents and pixels on every metric,

00:01:50.914 --> 00:01:53.510
even though the VAE reconstructs better.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.768
Semantics over reconstruction.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:02.767
We scaled to 2.1B and finetuned.

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:07.067
For generation, DINO reaches 0.88 on GenEval,

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:09.970
and SigLIP2 reaches 0.87.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.590
For understanding, the same SigLIP2 weights

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:15.815
reach 73.1 on VQAv2,

00:02:15.815 --> 00:02:18.471
with no LLM initialization.

00:02:18.521 --> 00:02:21.812
For joint generation, from noise in both streams,

00:02:21.812 --> 00:02:24.554
we get an image and a caption that describes it.

00:02:24.604 --> 00:02:26.312
What about joint reasoning?

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.662
In mazes with several shortest routes,

00:02:28.662 --> 00:02:31.131
JUICE draws a route and writes its moves.

00:02:31.181 --> 00:02:34.279
Sampled separately, the two often disagree.

00:02:34.329 --> 00:02:37.440
Coupled, each stream sees the other at every step,

00:02:37.729 --> 00:02:40.298
so whichever settles later can adapt.

00:02:40.429 --> 00:02:43.313
Joint success, where both are valid and match,

00:02:43.313 --> 00:02:47.989
rises from 54.6 to 93.7 percent:

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:51.165
close to the better fixed order, without choosing one.

00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:54.365
JUICE: one model to understand, generate,

00:02:54.365 --> 00:02:56.540
and coordinate images and text.
